{
  "image_name": "005f355318179ee698083ad947e7f9aa.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how chemicals accumulate and circulate through the plastic life cycle. On the left, plastics move from virgin feedstocks through resin manufacture, compounding, primary and secondary conversion, and finally into products. At each production step, additional substances are introduced: first monomers, catalysts, and stabilizers, then a wider range of additives such as fillers, nucleating agents, colourants, plasticizers, flame retardants, processing aids, antimicrobials, adhesives, and coatings. Small dark triangles appear to represent aggregated non-intentionally added substances.\n\nThe center and right side show what happens after products are made and used. During filling, retail, and consumer use, plastics can exchange contaminants with the environment and biota. After use, materials may be collected and sorted, then sent either to mechanical recycling, chemical recycling, or disposal. Mechanical recycling feeds recycled resins back into manufacturing, while chemical recycling returns material to recycled feedstocks and other chemicals. Shredding and washing are shown as intermediate steps that may not fully remove contaminants.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that plastics become chemically more complex over time, and that recycling can recirculate both intended additives and unintended contaminants through new products.",
